# Time-bounded dependency-advisory exceptions (COMPAT-03, issue #474).
#
# This is NOT another copy of the permanent, no-expiry ignore lists this repo
# already has (.trivyignore, .grype.yml, zap/dast.ignore,
# schemathesis/schemathesis.ignore, docker/cis-hardening.ignore,
# android/.mobsf, docs/security/citation-drift.ignore,
# docs/security/crypto-surface.ignore). Those exist for "the scanner is
# right that this pattern exists, but it does not apply to how we use this
# dependency" — a decision that can stand forever once written down.
#
# This file is for the different case dependency-upgrade-policy.md's
# "Security fixes" tier describes: a real advisory against a dependency we
# actually ship, that we currently CANNOT fix (no upstream release yet, the
# fix is blocked by another pin, a license question is still open) — not a
# false positive, an unresolved one. Recording it here instead of leaving it
# silent buys time, but not indefinitely: every entry carries an `expires`
# date, and `cd backend && go run ./cmd/depexceptions` fails once that date
# passes. An expired entry must be resolved (apply the fix) or renewed (a
# fresh dated entry with an updated reason) — it cannot just sit here.
#
# Format, one exception per line, pipe-delimited (`|`), no trailing `#`
# comment — the reason is its own field so it can safely mention "issue 474"
# or similar without colliding with a comment delimiter:
#
#   <advisory> | <ecosystem> | <package> | <first_opened> | <opened> | <expires> | <renewals> | <owner> | <reason>
#
#   advisory      GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx, a CVE-YYYY-NNNNN, or another advisory ID
#   ecosystem     one of: go, npm, gradle, docker, github-actions
#   package       the module/package name the advisory is against
#   first_opened  YYYY-MM-DD — the date this exception was FIRST recorded;
#                 never changes across renewals (that's what makes `renewals`
#                 and total age below meaningful)
#   opened        YYYY-MM-DD — the date of the CURRENT window; equals
#                 `first_opened` until the first renewal, then the date of the
#                 latest renewal
#   expires       YYYY-MM-DD — must be after `opened`, at most 90 days out
#                 (matches the deprecation-policy default period, MAINT-01,
#                 issue #490 — one project-wide "revisit this" cadence)
#   renewals      how many times this entry has been renewed — 0 the first
#                 time it's recorded, incremented by 1 on every renewal
#   owner         the GitHub username who recorded it and will revisit it
#   reason        why it cannot be fixed right now, non-empty
#
# To renew an expiring entry: bump `opened`/`expires` to a fresh 90-day
# window, increment `renewals`, leave `first_opened` untouched, and update
# `reason` to say what changed since the last look. Issue #942: once
# `renewals` reaches 2 (i.e. on the entry's third recording — roughly nine
# months unresolved), `cd backend && go run ./cmd/depexceptions` additionally
# requires `reason` to contain an `escalated:` note — an unfixable CVE cannot
# just accumulate quiet 90-day renewals forever; from that point someone has
# to look at it again and write down a real decision (still waiting on
# upstream, accepted risk pending a rewrite, whatever it is) rather than
# rubber-stamping another extension.
#
# Example (not a real exception — delete before adding a real one):
#   GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx | npm | example-pkg | 2026-09-04 | 2026-09-04 | 2026-11-03 | 0 | drew | no upstream fix yet; tracked at github.com/example/example-pkg/issues/1
#
# Currently empty: no known advisory against a shipped dependency is
# unfixable right now. Add a line above this one, following the format, when
# one comes up.
